    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a clear objective: maximize stolen base opportunities and leverage favorable matchups against high-xERA starters. **Mickey Moniak** leads the charge as our strongest play with a 69.98 efficiency score, benefiting from an elite +19% boost against the vulnerable Eric Lauer and a favorable park factor, though he carries a 37% rain risk that bears watching. Right behind him, **Isaac Paredes** (68.21) is positioned to capitalize on a soft matchup against George Klassen, aided by a +19% SP skill advantage and a strong dynamic platoon edge. We are also leaning heavily into our speed-focused assets; **Corbin Carroll** (65.40) and **Jose Caballero** (60.34) are high-priority starts, specifically because both face pitchers with slow deliveries, creating a prime window to pad our scarcest category, SB.\n\nThe decision-making process was tight at the corner infield spots, with **Josh Naylor** (44.10) narrowly missing the starting cut. While **Josh Naylor** projects reasonably well, his efficiency was suppressed by a poor matchup against the tough Kyle Harrison and a lack of the \"elite speed\" profile we prioritize in our scoring model. Consequently, we are running **Vinnie Pasquantino** (60.28) at 1B, as his +14% platoon advantage against Jack Perkins provided a more compelling statistical ceiling. Our lineup remains flexible pending official announcements, particularly for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Brice Turang**, and **Max Muncy**, but we remain confident that this configuration optimally balances our scarcity needs with the best available park and pitcher-skill edges. We continue to monitor the status of **James Wood** on the IL, alongside reserves like **Trevor Story**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, **Cole Young**, and **Henry Bolte**, keeping a close eye on any late roster shifts.",
